% ============================================================================
%  Bibliography_base.bib — INTENTIONALLY CARRIES NO ENTRIES.
%
%  CONSOLIDATED 2026-08-03. This project keeps exactly ONE file with entries:
%      quality_reports/literature/panel-npiv-discrete/references.bib
%  Its two former entries (Angrist & Pischke 2009; Imbens 2004) were moved there
%  verbatim, in a marked block. Nothing was lost and neither was ever cited.
%
%  WHY THE FILE STILL EXISTS AND MUST NOT BE DELETED.
%  paper/preambles/preamble.tex:160 declares \addbibresource{Bibliography_base.bib},
%  and that preamble is SHARED ACROSS PROJECTS — its own comment says naming one
%  project's bibliography there "would break the rest". So the declaration stays,
%  which means the file must exist and parse: biber aborts on a missing resource,
%  and an aborted biber leaves a zero-byte .bbl and renders every citation as bare
%  key text in a document that still exits 0. That failure mode has already cost
%  this project weeks, which is why render.sh and build.sh check for it directly.
%
%  WHERE TO ADD AN ENTRY.
%  In the PROJECT file named above, never here. Two files both resolving meant two
%  places to look and two places to get wrong; an entry added here would resolve,
%  which is precisely what made the split hard to notice rather than easy.
%
%  N-21 STILL APPLIES WHEREVER YOU ADD ONE. Copy the BibTeX verbatim from the
%  author's master bibliographies under ~/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/**/*.bib
%  when the work is already there. Never compose an entry from recall: this
%  project has produced six wrong locators that way, every one of them plausible.
%
%  A FORK STARTING FROM THE TEMPLATE should either put its own entries here and
%  delete this notice, or do what this project did and point at a per-project
%  file. Either is fine. Two live files is what is not.
% ============================================================================
